 dict(pmid='', doi='10.1038/s41612-026-01583-6', journal='npj Clim Atmos Sci', short='Langford cooking dominant primary PM1 eddy covariance London Beijing Delhi 2026', title='Traffic and combustion emission controls leave cooking as a dominant local source of primary PM1 in megacities', sub=corpus.EXPO, design='Eddy covariance flux measurement, method development', pm='Chemically resolved non-refractory PM1 fluxes (aerosol mass spectrometry + eddy covariance)', geo='Global / multi-region (London, Beijing, Delhi)', endpoint='Source-attributed primary PM1 emission fluxes', tier='A', n="Eddy-covariance fluxes with aerosol mass spectrometry and a new source-attribution method in three megacity neighbourhoods. In Delhi, traffic and combustion dominate primary PM1 emission. In London and Beijing, cooking is the largest local primary source at mealtimes: up to 83% of midday and 87% of early-evening emission in London, 61% and 76% in Beijing. Cooking is absent from national, European and global inventories, though the London Atmospheric Emissions Inventory's estimate matched. Primary emission sources differ from those controlling ambient PM1 mass, which is secondary-dominated. Limitation: one flux footprint per city and non-refractory PM1 only (no BC or dust). Relevance: high - source-resolved flux benchmarks for inventory checks; cooking plumes are a known optical-sensor artefact at street level."),
 dict(pmid='42916439', doi='10.1021/acs.est.6c07515', journal='Environ Sci Technol', short='Sun respirable mining dust natural killer cell dysfunction 2026', title='Respirable Particulate Matter Induces Natural Killer Cell Dysfunction via Oxidative Stress-Mediated Lysosomal and Mitochondrial Injury: Implications for Mineral-Rich Mining Dust Exposure.', sub=corpus.MECH, design='In vitro', pm='Compositionally distinct respirable PM (silica/silicate, iron-bearing, carbonaceous mine dusts)', geo='Not stated in record', endpoint='NK-cell viability, cytokines, perforin/granzyme B, K562 killing', tier='B', n='In-vitro exposure of NK cells to respirable mineral dusts with a K562 target-cell challenge. Injury was dose- and composition-dependent: apoptosis/necrosis, shifted cytokines, altered perforin/granzyme B function and impaired killing. Silica- and silicate-rich dusts were worst; iron-bearing and carbonaceous dusts produced distinct oxidative patterns. Mitochondrial depolarisation, lower mitochondrial mass and lysosomal deacidification converged across dusts; ascorbic acid partially restored organelle function and IFN-gamma. Doses and their relation to inhaled lung burden are not given in the abstract. Limitation: an isolated cell line in submerged culture, no lung-burden dosimetry, so the proposed adverse outcome pathway to lung cancer is inferential. Relevance: low - composition (silica fraction) matters, which mass sensors cannot resolve. Note: this DOI was held from the 3 Oct issue as unauthored and is now carried here.'),
 dict(pmid='', doi='10.1021/acs.est.6c12991', journal='Environ Sci Technol', short='Liang emergency haze controls causal ML synthetic control Shijiazhuang 2026', title='Evaluating Emergency Emission Controls During an Unprecedented Regional Haze Event Using Causal Machine Learning', sub=corpus.BURD, design='Augmented synthetic control + weather normalisation', pm='Monitor PM2.5, PM10, SO2, NO2, CO; PM2.5 source apportionment', geo='China (Shijiazhuang)', endpoint='Intervention-attributable emission strength and concentration change', tier='B', n="Three-step causal evaluation (meteorological normalisation, augmented synthetic control, ML prediction) of Shijiazhuang's 45-day 'Sharp Sword' campaign in 2016. Local emission strength fell 4.3% (PM2.5), 4.8% (PM10), 15.8% (SO2), 5.8% (NO2), 15.3% (CO); concentrations fell 42.1 ug/m3 (14.8%) for PM2.5 and 47.4 ug/m3 (12.4%) for PM10. Independent PMF showed vehicle, steelmaking and coal contributions 4.1%, 11.0% and 21.7% lower than in 2017. PM2.5 still averaged 243.2 ug/m3 during the campaign. Limitation: donor-city synthetic controls share the regional haze and possibly spillover controls, biasing the effect toward zero; no uncertainty intervals in the abstract. Relevance: medium - a transferable counterfactual framework for evaluating interventions from network data."),
